WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.280
Let’s talk about gene editing and its potential to change lives.

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:08.800
It’s because of CRISPR — a technology that’s not even 10 

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:12.400
years old and can basically perform surgery on our genes.

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:15.920
The inventors got a Nobel Prize for it in 2020.

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:20.080
CRISPR fans say it’ll fight diseases and improve crops.

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:24.440
Other people? Well, Hollywood tapped into their fears a long time ago.

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:33.800
At least one scientist has crossed the line.

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:42.320
So how does gene editing actually work? 

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:44.560
Can CRISPR be used to cure disease?

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:49.160
And how far should we be allowed to go in transforming the human race?

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:02.560
Genes are the blueprint for making everything the body needs.

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:05.280
And human beings have more than 20,000 of them.

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:10.600
Genes are made up of DNA and that instructs the cell how to make a protein.

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:14.920
Those proteins carry out the functions of the body and determine the way we look.

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:17.920
But some gene variations can make us sick.

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:22.600
Scientists, though, may now have the unprecedented power to change those genes.

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:26.760
It’s all because of the work done by two Nobel Prize winning scientists:

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:29.720
Emmanuelle Charpentier and Jennifer Doudna. 

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:34.240
What they discovered has unleashed a whole new era of genetic engineering 

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:36.240
that’s advancing just about every day.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:39.440
The tool is called CRISPR–Cas9.

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:43.520
And like so often happens in science they discovered it by chance.

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:48.000
Charpentier and Doudna were studying how bacteria protect themselves from viruses 

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:50.360
because, yes, bacteria get sick too.

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:54.040
They found that bacteria have a part of their DNA called CRISPR

00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:57.200
that stores tiny pieces of a virus’s DNA.

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:02.520
A copy of that bacterial CRISPR DNA is made the next time the virus comes back.

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:06.880
Small parts of the copy then join up with a protein called Cas9.

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:10.440
So CRISPR–Cas9 goes looking for that matching bit of DNA

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:14.320
in the virus, latches onto it and through a chemical reaction

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:17.240
it snips the viral DNA to destroy it.

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:22.000
It’s why scientists describe CRISPR–Cas9 as a pair of genetic scissors.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:25.080
So Charpentier and Doudna then thought:

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:28.080
Why can’t we harness that same natural process 

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:31.360
but programme it to cut out genes that are faulty 

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:33.600
and replace them with healthy ones.

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:35.640
That was the lightbulb moment.

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:52.000
It’s simple to use and cheap and that makes it revolutionary.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:55.640
Because it could treat, perhaps even cure, genetic diseases

00:02:55.640 --> 00:02:59.880
like muscular dystrophy, cystic fibrosis, even cancer.

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:03.440
And a lot of attention has been on sickle cell disease.

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:08.440
In fact scientists have already used it on a woman named Victoria Gray.

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:13.440
She was the first person in the US to receive a CRISPR-based treatment for the disease.

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:18.920
Doctors infused her with more than 2 billion of her own genetically edited bone marrow cells.

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:22.840
More than a year later almost all her symptoms are gone.

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:27.760
Just to be clear — CRISPR is being used here like any other medical treatment.

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:32.400
Any genetic changes stay with that patient. They aren’t passed on to their children. 

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:36.760
They’re not hereditary changes that affect the human gene pool forever.

00:03:36.760 --> 00:03:41.960
That’s because scientists are fixing faulty genes in somatic cells — basically all the cells in

00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:44.800
our bodies except the single-cell sperm and egg.

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:49.840
And it’s important to point out the patient is an adult who’s consented to the treatment.

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:55.200
But some are suggesting using CRISPR on early embryos to make the genetic fix. 

00:03:55.200 --> 00:04:00.680
And for a lot of safety and societal reasons scientists say that’s a step too far.

00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:03.720
But that didn’t stop a Chinese scientist from going there.

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:13.800
In 2018 He Jiankui announced the birth of the world’s first CRISPR-modified babies — 

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:16.400
twin girls known only as Lulu and Nana. 

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:25.600
There was outrage.

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:48.640
He’d altered a gene to try to protect them from HIV because their father has AIDS.

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:52.000
But he did it while the girls were still in a petri dish.

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:56.240
And that’s why his use of CRISPR technology is a problem.

00:04:56.240 --> 00:04:59.120
He genetically altered what are called germ-line cells:

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:05.160
the sperm, the egg, or in the case of the twin girls, a fertilised egg at the single-cell stage.

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:09.200
Changes to DNA here appear in every cell in a person’s body. 

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:11.360
You might say that’s an efficient fix

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:16.520
but those genetic changes are permanent and are passed onto future generations.

00:05:16.520 --> 00:05:19.280
Plus, this is unchartered territory. 

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:26.720
Scientists say there’s a very real risk that an edit could cause a mutation in the genome with side effects that we just can’t predict. 

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:28.960
There’s also the issue of consent.

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:32.640
The girls obviously had no say in the changes made to their bodies.

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:54.080
That experiment alone raised the spectre of designer babies, making people smarter, stronger, maybe more attractive.

00:06:04.960 --> 00:06:07.000
So what’s being done to regulate it?

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:12.320
Seventy-five countries already prohibit the use of CRISPR in human reproduction.  

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:16.240
But many scientists and governments agree more rules are needed.  

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:21.440
Right now we have an international commission that’s started to draft some guidelines.

00:06:41.320 --> 00:06:44.880
None of that, though, has stopped scientists from experimenting with CRISPR — 

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:47.240
in plants and animals at least.

00:06:47.240 --> 00:06:50.400
So what happened to the man who did interfere?

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:56.720
Well, He Jiankui was sentenced to three years in prison and banned from working on reproductive medicine for life.

00:06:57.320 --> 00:07:01.840
And the twin girls? No one really knows where they are or whether they’re healthy.

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:05.640
We don’t even know who else in the world is trying this technology.

00:07:07.240 --> 00:07:12.440
There’s a good chance someone will misuse it and potentially change the human race. 

00:07:12.440 --> 00:07:15.640
That’s enough to want to bury this technology.

00:07:15.640 --> 00:07:21.680
But CRISPR has enormous potential to transform some people’s lives without hurting anyone else. 

00:07:21.680 --> 00:07:25.240
That’s a power people just won’t want to ignore.

